1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,151
Marco sent the team
these two blocks of commands.

2
00:00:04,471 --> 00:00:08,532
They start the CareOneX
voice app on his laptop.

3
00:00:08,852 --> 00:00:13,322
If they look like a wall of noise,
that is normal.

4
00:00:13,567 --> 00:00:18,428
By the end of this video,
every line will mean something.

5
00:00:18,748 --> 00:00:25,984
Each one turns out to be a small lesson
about how the whole system fits together.

6
00:00:26,233 --> 00:00:27,506
One warning first.

7
00:00:27,826 --> 00:00:31,112
We cannot log into the team's AWS account.

8
00:00:31,432 --> 00:00:37,486
So everything here is learned from the code
on GitHub and the team's notes.

9
00:00:37,806 --> 00:00:42,131
Each claim carries a tag,
and when we are guessing,

10
00:00:42,131 --> 00:00:45,438
the tag says INFERRED, with our reason.

11
00:00:46,483 --> 00:00:48,851
The two blocks start two programs.

12
00:00:49,171 --> 00:00:52,768
Terminal 1 starts retrieve,
a small search server.

13
00:00:53,088 --> 00:00:57,543
Terminal 2 starts voice,
the program you actually talk to.

14
00:00:57,783 --> 00:01:01,894
Voice listens to your microphone
and plays the reply.

15
00:01:02,214 --> 00:01:06,817
When it needs a fact,
it asks retrieve, on the same laptop.

16
00:01:07,137 --> 00:01:11,033
Both programs also talk
to Amazon's cloud, AWS,

17
00:01:11,033 --> 00:01:15,178
where the AI models
and the document library live.

18
00:01:15,416 --> 00:01:20,779
A terminal is a window
where you type commands instead of clicking.

19
00:01:21,099 --> 00:01:24,038
Marco is on Windows, using PowerShell.

20
00:01:24,358 --> 00:01:28,286
We can tell
from the way his lines are written.

21
00:01:29,333 --> 00:01:32,422
Line 1 is cd, short for change directory.

22
00:01:32,742 --> 00:01:36,236
It moves the terminal
into the project folder.

23
00:01:36,556 --> 00:01:41,750
Every later command looks
for its files relative to this folder.

24
00:01:42,000 --> 00:01:46,015
The folder is Marco's
own copy of the team's code,

25
00:01:46,015 --> 00:01:48,423
with his newest changes in it.

26
00:01:48,743 --> 00:01:49,908
We infer that,

27
00:01:49,908 --> 00:01:56,227
because the next commands expect exactly
the folders of the team repository.

28
00:01:57,267 --> 00:02:00,430
Most of the other lines look like this:

29
00:02:00,430 --> 00:02:04,405
dollar sign, env, colon,
a name, equals, a value.

30
00:02:04,725 --> 00:02:07,874
Each one sets an environment variable.

31
00:02:08,117 --> 00:02:13,190
Think of environment variables
as sticky notes on the terminal window.

32
00:02:13,510 --> 00:02:17,292
Every program started
from that window can read them.

33
00:02:17,612 --> 00:02:21,140
The CareOneX code reads
its settings this way,

34
00:02:21,140 --> 00:02:25,512
so nothing about Marco's laptop
is written into the code.

35
00:02:25,750 --> 00:02:28,861
The notes belong to one window only.

36
00:02:29,181 --> 00:02:34,095
That is why both blocks set
the same AWS lines again.

37
00:02:35,133 --> 00:02:40,782
An AWS account is the team's
own fenced-off corner of Amazon's cloud.

38
00:02:41,102 --> 00:02:46,154
Everything CareOneX uses lives inside it,
and it has one bill.

39
00:02:46,474 --> 00:02:52,528
The team's notes show a budget of 30 dollars
a month for the AI models.

40
00:02:52,766 --> 00:02:54,405
Nobody shares a password.

41
00:02:54,725 --> 00:02:58,783
Each teammate signs
in through a single sign-on portal,

42
00:02:58,783 --> 00:03:02,692
called SSO,
with their own email and a second factor.

43
00:03:03,012 --> 00:03:04,253
After signing in,

44
00:03:04,253 --> 00:03:08,267
everyone gets the same set of permissions,
named AC215.

45
00:03:08,587 --> 00:03:12,385
It covers the AI models,
the course's own storage,

46
00:03:12,385 --> 00:03:14,156
and the knowledge base,

47
00:03:14,156 --> 00:03:17,853
and nothing
in the company's production systems.

48
00:03:18,100 --> 00:03:22,642
A profile is a named entry
in a settings file on the laptop.

49
00:03:22,962 --> 00:03:26,789
"careonex-team" says:
log in through that portal,

50
00:03:26,789 --> 00:03:30,693
into the team account,
with the AC215 permissions.

51
00:03:31,013 --> 00:03:35,436
The command aws sso login opens a browser,
you approve,

52
00:03:35,436 --> 00:03:40,019
and the laptop receives temporary keys
that last 8 hours.

53
00:03:40,266 --> 00:03:45,484
The CareOneX code finds those temporary keys
through the profile name.

54
00:03:45,804 --> 00:03:47,145
When they expire,

55
00:03:47,145 --> 00:03:53,136
the voice program stops with a one-line
message telling you to log in again.

56
00:03:54,183 --> 00:03:58,652
AWS runs data centers
in many regions around the world.

57
00:03:58,972 --> 00:04:02,243
Each region is its own copy of the cloud.

58
00:04:02,563 --> 00:04:06,963
The team's models
and storage are all in us-east-1,

59
00:04:06,963 --> 00:04:12,483
in Northern Virginia,
so every program must be told to go there.

60
00:04:13,517 --> 00:04:16,892
Now the strangest line: set HOME to HOME.

61
00:04:17,212 --> 00:04:19,619
It looks like it does nothing.

62
00:04:19,939 --> 00:04:22,314
It fixes a Windows problem.

63
00:04:22,550 --> 00:04:27,437
The retrieve program will run
inside a container, a sealed box.

64
00:04:27,757 --> 00:04:31,586
To use your login,
the box needs your keys folder.

65
00:04:31,906 --> 00:04:37,682
The team's Docker file finds that folder
through a variable named HOME.

66
00:04:37,917 --> 00:04:40,346
On a Mac, HOME is always set.

67
00:04:40,666 --> 00:04:42,558
On Windows it is not,

68
00:04:42,558 --> 00:04:48,594
so the box would look for keys
in an empty path and fail to log in.

69
00:04:48,914 --> 00:04:55,192
The line copies PowerShell's own home
folder into the HOME sticky note.

70
00:04:55,512 --> 00:05:01,194
Only terminal 1 needs it,
because only terminal 1 uses Docker.

71
00:05:02,233 --> 00:05:07,721
A Knowledge Base is an Amazon service
that stores the team's documents

72
00:05:07,721 --> 00:05:12,346
as small searchable passages,
and searches them on request.

73
00:05:12,666 --> 00:05:17,365
Every knowledge base has an ID,
a short code like this one.

74
00:05:17,600 --> 00:05:21,776
The retrieve code looks
for this sticky note first.

75
00:05:22,096 --> 00:05:28,025
If it is missing, it reads the ID
from a file in the team's storage,

76
00:05:28,025 --> 00:05:32,732
written there by the container
that built the library.

77
00:05:32,966 --> 00:05:34,284
Why set it by hand?

78
00:05:34,604 --> 00:05:39,272
We had guessed two reasons:
to save one read from storage,

79
00:05:39,272 --> 00:05:43,376
or to point retrieve
at a different knowledge base.

80
00:05:43,696 --> 00:05:47,553
Marco's branch,
pushed later that day, settles it.

81
00:05:47,873 --> 00:05:51,640
This ID is one
of three test libraries he built

82
00:05:51,640 --> 00:05:54,925
to compare ways of cutting the documents.

83
00:05:55,245 --> 00:05:59,512
Without this line,
retrieve would use the main library.

84
00:05:59,832 --> 00:06:02,126
Episode 6 explains his test.

85
00:06:03,167 --> 00:06:08,466
Docker packs a program
with everything it needs into an image,

86
00:06:08,466 --> 00:06:10,517
like a frozen lunch box.

87
00:06:10,837 --> 00:06:13,480
A container is that box, running.

88
00:06:13,800 --> 00:06:19,204
It runs the same on Marco's laptop,
on yours, and on a server.

89
00:06:19,450 --> 00:06:24,652
Docker compose reads the team's file
that describes all the containers.

90
00:06:24,972 --> 00:06:26,493
The word up starts one.

91
00:06:26,813 --> 00:06:32,398
The option build first rebuilds its image,
so Marco's newest code is inside.

92
00:06:32,718 --> 00:06:35,940
And retrieve picks just the search server.

93
00:06:36,184 --> 00:06:40,338
Inside the box,
retrieve becomes a small web server.

94
00:06:40,658 --> 00:06:43,883
It listens on port 8080, a numbered door.

95
00:06:44,203 --> 00:06:49,832
The Docker file connects that door
to the same door on the laptop,

96
00:06:49,832 --> 00:06:52,221
so other programs can knock.

97
00:06:53,267 --> 00:06:56,612
In terminal 2,
the first new note tells voice

98
00:06:56,612 --> 00:06:57,950
where retrieve is.

99
00:06:58,270 --> 00:07:04,184
127.0.0.1 always means this same computer,
and 8080 is the door retrieve opened.

100
00:07:04,504 --> 00:07:10,662
Without this note, the voice app answers
that the knowledge base is unavailable.

101
00:07:10,900 --> 00:07:13,860
The next note picks how voice searches.

102
00:07:14,180 --> 00:07:18,781
When we first looked,
it was nowhere in the code on GitHub.

103
00:07:19,101 --> 00:07:23,046
Marco's branch,
pushed later that day, explains it.

104
00:07:23,366 --> 00:07:28,951
Feedback runs a first search,
may add one more, and merges the results.

105
00:07:29,271 --> 00:07:31,493
Baseline runs one search only.

106
00:07:31,813 --> 00:07:36,003
Feedback is also the default
when the note is missing.

107
00:07:36,323 --> 00:07:38,702
Episode 6 shows what it does.

108
00:07:39,750 --> 00:07:42,265
The last line is the longest.

109
00:07:42,585 --> 00:07:44,475
Let's take it apart.

110
00:07:44,717 --> 00:07:50,412
uv is a tool that sets up the exact Python
and libraries a project needs,

111
00:07:50,412 --> 00:07:53,376
then runs a command inside that setup.

112
00:07:53,696 --> 00:07:58,938
Python 3.12 is the oldest version
Amazon's streaming library accepts.

113
00:07:59,258 --> 00:08:03,469
And the directory option says:
use the voice project.

114
00:08:03,717 --> 00:08:06,421
extra mic adds the microphone library.

115
00:08:06,741 --> 00:08:12,132
It is optional on purpose:
a container has no microphone or speakers,

116
00:08:12,132 --> 00:08:16,586
so the voice program runs directly
on the laptop instead.

117
00:08:16,833 --> 00:08:23,101
Finally, the quoted part is a tiny Python
program that starts the voice session.

118
00:08:23,421 --> 00:08:27,136
The team's instructions
use a shorter command,

119
00:08:27,136 --> 00:08:31,498
but that one first checks
for a kind of saved key file

120
00:08:31,498 --> 00:08:34,325
that an SSO login may never create.

121
00:08:34,645 --> 00:08:38,148
Calling the session directly
skips that check.

122
00:08:38,468 --> 00:08:42,373
That is our inference;
it fits the code exactly.

123
00:08:43,417 --> 00:08:45,806
Put together, it looks like this.

124
00:08:46,126 --> 00:08:48,754
Both programs use your 8-hour login.

125
00:08:49,074 --> 00:08:56,051
You speak, voice streams it to Nova 2 Sonic,
Nova asks for a fact, voice asks retrieve,

126
00:08:56,051 --> 00:09:01,264
retrieve searches the knowledge base,
and Nova speaks the answer.

127
00:09:01,500 --> 00:09:06,499
Here is every new word
from this video on one card.

128
00:09:06,819 --> 00:09:10,298
Pause here if you want to keep it.

129
00:09:10,534 --> 00:09:17,973
Next, the big picture: what CareOneX is for,
and who on the team built which part.

